The Human-English defines a continuous lattice \(D\) to be a projection of a continuous lattice \(D'\) when continuous maps \(i : D \to D'\) and \(j : D' \to D\) satisfy both \(j \circ i = \mathrm{id}_D\) and \(i \circ j \sqsubseteq \mathrm{id}_{D'}\); for continuous \(f : D \to D''\) the composite \(\tilde f = f \circ j\) then extends \(f\), and every other continuous extension \(f'\) obeys \(\tilde f = f \circ j = f' \circ i \circ j \sqsubseteq f'\), so \(\tilde f\) is the least element of \([D' \to D'']\), the correspondence \(f \mapsto \tilde f\) is continuous, and, in the opening sentence, composition \((f,g) \mapsto f \circ g\) is continuous in both function variables. The AI English of `IsContinuousLatticeRetraction` is a Scott-map inclusion \(D \to D'\) and a Scott-map retraction \(D' \to D\) with the retraction after the inclusion equal to the identity, and `IsContinuousLatticeProjection` adds that the inclusion after the retraction lies below the identity, which is the same pair of laws with the same variance. `ScottMapRetraction` and `ScottMapProjection`, labeled there as the two halves of Proposition 3.7, send \(f\) to \(i_1 \circ f \circ j_0\) and \(f'\) to \(j_1 \circ f' \circ i_0\), obtain the identity law by cancelling the two retraction equations under extensionality, and obtain the function-space inequality by the deflation \(i_1 \circ j_1 \sqsubseteq \mathrm{id}\) followed by monotonicity of \(f'\) applied to \(i_0 \circ j_0 \sqsubseteq \mathrm{id}\); that monotonicity is the same move that would put \(f' \circ i \circ j\) below \(f'\), but neither gloss states the identity \(\tilde f = f' \circ i \circ j\) or the conclusion that \(f \circ j\) is the least extension of \(f\). Continuity of \(f \mapsto i_1 \circ f \circ j_0\) is present because that inclusion is a Scott map. I checked `IsProjection` and `isProjection_iff` (Definition 3.11: a Scott endomap equal to its iterate and lying below the identity), `projection`, `min`, and `con_` (Proposition 3.13: the constant-map embedding and evaluation at bottom exhibit \(D\) as a projection of the Scott-map space, since the value at bottom of a constant map is that constant and the constant map with value \(f(\bot)\) lies below \(f\)), `converseRetr`, `converseRetr_incl`, `incl_converseRetr_le`, and `converseProjection` (the candidate \(j(x') = \bigsqcup\{x \mid i(x) \le x'\}\) satisfies \(j \circ i = \mathrm{id}\) and \(i \circ j \le \mathrm{id}\)), the pointwise-order files `le`, `le_def`, `le_refl'`, `le_trans'`, and `le_antisymm'`, `idMap` and `comp_apply`, and the fixed-point files `fix`, `fix_eq`, `fix_le`, and `fixMap`: none swaps the two composites or reverses \(i \circ j \sqsubseteq \mathrm{id}\). Missing are the degrading identity and the least-extension conclusion, and also joint continuity of composition, since `comp_apply` only says that \((f \circ g)(x) = f(g(x))\). Extra are the complete lattice of idempotent deflations (`instCompleteLattice`, `isProjection_sSup`, `isProjection_directedSup`, Proposition 3.12) and the step-function, curry, and pointwise-topology infrastructure. **Theorem:** partial — the two equations of Definition 3.6 are formalized, and the least-extension claim together with joint continuity of composition are not. **Proof:** partial — the calculation \(\tilde f = f' \circ i \circ j \sqsubseteq f'\) is absent; the monotonicity step appears only inside the proof that the induced function-space pair is a projection.
